---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const trussCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'P*x/(4*(1 - x*x/16))', label: 'bending moment (simply supported)', color: '#2563eb', width: 2.4 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 7.9, label: 'position along the span (m)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 30, label: 'bending moment (kN·m), P = 10 kN' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 330,
};

const staticsCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Free-body first', note: 'Isolate the structure, replace the world with forces. Every analysis begins with an honest free-body diagram.' },
    { math: '2 · Equilibrium: three equations', note: 'ΣF = 0 in two directions and ΣM = 0 about any point. Two-dimensional statics has exactly three equations per body.' },
    { math: '3 · Statically determinate or not', note: 'If the three equations find every reaction, the structure is determinate. If not, you need material properties — that is indeterminate analysis.' },
    { math: '4 · Internal forces appear', note: 'Cut the structure and equilibrium still holds: each cut exposes axial force, shear and bending moment that the section must carry.' },
    { math: '5 · Dynamics when things move', note: 'Add inertia (F = ma) and moving loads become dynamic: impact factors, resonance, and the reason soldiers break step on bridges.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/civil-engineering/foundational/statics-and-dynamics-for-civil-engineers">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Civil engineers make an unusual promise: this structure will hold, for decades, through
    storms, crowds, traffic and earthquakes — and we can prove it on paper before a single
    bucket of concrete is poured. The proof rests on a handful of ideas that fit in one lecture:
    bodies at rest are in equilibrium, equilibrium is three equations per body in two
    dimensions, and cutting a structure open reveals the internal forces that every square
    centimetre of it must survive. That is statics. When loads move — trucks, wind gusts,
    marching crowds, earthquake waves — inertia joins the picture and statics grows into
    dynamics.
  </p>
  <p>
    The everyday analogy is a set of scales. A bridge deck loaded with traffic is a giant
    balance: the loads push down, the supports push up, and the two must match exactly or the
    bridge accelerates — which, for a successful bridge, means never. What makes the subject
    rich is that matching the totals is only the first question. A bridge can have its vertical
    forces perfectly balanced and still fail, because some internal member is quietly carrying
    far more than its share. The engineer's task is to trace where every newton flows: down
    through the deck, along the beams, into the columns, and finally into the ground.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is why the free-body diagram is the subject's signature move. Isolate the piece you
    care about, cut it free from the world, and replace everything you cut away with the forces
    it was exerting. Suddenly an abstract question — is this truss safe? — becomes a concrete
    one: here are the forces, here are three equations, solve. Everything in this chapter is a
    variation on that single move, applied first to whole structures, then to imagined cuts
    through them, and finally to bodies in motion where the equations gain a right-hand side.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from free-body diagram to internal forces" widget="equationStep" config={staticsCfg} />

  <h2>Trusses and frames</h2>
  <p>
    A truss is equilibrium made visible. It is a skeleton of straight bars joined at their ends
    into triangles, and its genius is a single assumption: the joints are pins, so each bar
    carries force only along its own axis. A bar can pull (tension) or push (compression), but
    it cannot bend. That turns a daunting tangle of steel into pure arithmetic, because a
    two-force member exerts at each joint a force of known direction.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <em>method of joints</em> exploits this one pin at a time. Take a simple example: two
    rafters meeting at an apex, joined by a horizontal tie, with a 10 kN load hanging at the
    apex. At the apex joint there are two unknown bar forces and exactly two useful equilibrium
    equations (sum of forces horizontal and vertical equals zero). If the rafters sit at 30
    degrees to the horizontal, vertical equilibrium reads: two rafter forces × sin 30° must
    carry 10 kN, so each rafter pushes with 10 kN in compression. Horizontal equilibrium then
    forces the tie to pull with 10 kN × cos 30° ≈ 8.7 kN in tension. Three members, one joint,
    three sentences of algebra — and a designer already knows which bars need buckling checks
    (the compression ones) and which need only strength (the tie).
  </p>
  <p>
    Why triangles? Because a triangle is the only polygon whose shape is fixed by its side
    lengths. A rectangle of hinged bars can shear into a parallelogram; a hinged triangle
    cannot deform without changing a bar's length. Trusses are therefore built from triangles,
    and the pattern repeats at every scale — from a roof rafter assembly to the Forth Bridge
    cantilevers. <em>Frames</em> are the generalisation: structures whose members carry forces
    at more than two points, so individual members can bend. Every joint then has more unknowns
    than equations, and the analysis must cut through members and use internal forces — which
    is exactly where beam theory takes over.
  </p>

  <h2>Centroids and moment of inertia</h2>
  <p>
    Two geometric properties decide how a cross-section behaves, and both are worth
    understanding physically rather than memorising. The <em>centroid</em> is the section's
    balance point: the point where the area would balance on a pin. Loads passed through it
    produce pure compression or tension; the same loads applied off-centre add bending. The
    <em>second moment of area</em> I — often called, loosely, the moment of inertia of the
    section — measures how far the section's area sits from the axis it bends about, each bit
    of area weighted by the square of its distance.
  </p>
  <p>
    The squaring is what makes I so powerful. For a rectangle of width b and depth h,
    I = bh³/12 about the horizontal axis. Take a 100 mm × 200 mm timber joist:
    I = 100 × 200³ ÷ 12 ≈ 66.7 × 10⁶ mm⁴. Now stand it the other way — 200 mm wide, 100 mm
    deep — and I collapses to 16.7 × 10⁶ mm⁴, four times less. Same plank, same area, same
    weight: four times less stiffness, purely because of orientation. Going further, doubling
    the depth from 200 mm to 400 mm multiplies I by 2³ = 8. This is the whole secret of the
    I-beam and the box girder: put material as far from the bending axis as practical, and
    connect it with a thin web that mostly keeps it in place. Bending stress follows
    σ = My ÷ I — stress grows with distance y from the neutral axis (which is why flanges,
    at maximum y, do the work) and shrinks in direct proportion to I.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "more area means more stiffness." Not so — geometry
    dominates. A thin steel rule edge-on is far harder to bend than a much heavier plank laid
    flat, and a hollow tube beats a solid rod of the same area. Area controls axial strength;
    the distribution of area (I) controls bending, and bending is what kills most beams.
  </p>

  <h2>Beams and the bending moment diagram</h2>
  <p>
    Cut a loaded beam at any station and equilibrium still holds for the piece on either side;
    the cut must therefore transmit an internal shear force and an internal bending moment.
    Plot that moment along the span and you have the single most informative drawing in
    structural engineering. For a simply supported beam of span L carrying a central point
    load P, statics gives a triangular diagram: zero at both supports, rising linearly to the
    peak PL/4 at mid-span. With P = 10 kN and L = 8 m, the peak is 10 × 8 ÷ 4 = 20 kN·m.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="bending moment under a central point load" widget="plotter" config={trussCfg} />

  <p>
    The diagram is not a formality — it is the design. The peak tells you the maximum demand
    the worst section must carry; the zero points tell you where connections can be lighter;
    the shape tells you where to add depth or reinforcement. Distribute the same load
    uniformly instead of concentrating it, and the peak drops from PL/4 to wL²/8 — with
    wL = P that is P·L ÷ 8, half the central-load value. Real failure investigations often
    reduce to a bending moment diagram drawn after the fact: the load was heavier, or in a
    different place, than the diagram the designer had assumed. Every load case draws its own
    diagram, and the structure must survive the envelope of all of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Kinematics and kinetics: when loads move</h2>
  <p>
    Statics assumes the structure never accelerates. <em>Kinematics</em> is the descriptive
    layer that comes first: position, velocity and acceleration, and how they relate (velocity
    is the rate of change of position; acceleration the rate of change of velocity).
    <em>Kinetics</em> is the causal layer: Newton's second law, force equals mass times
    acceleration, which converts acceleration history into the forces the structure must
    resist. In two-dimensional dynamics, three statics equations become three equations of
    motion, with the mass times acceleration terms playing the role the reactions played at
    rest.
  </p>
  <p>
    Civil engineers meet kinetics through moving loads, and the effects are rarely intuitive.
    A truck rolling across a bridge loads it more heavily than the same truck parked, because
    the deck must also supply the forces that deflect the vehicle's suspension and, in an
    impact, arrest its motion — codes capture this with dynamic amplification factors, often
    on the order of 1.3 for highway bridges. Deeper still, every structure has natural
    frequencies at which it prefers to swing, set by its stiffness and mass. When a load's
    rhythm matches a natural frequency, each cycle pumps more energy in and the response grows
    — resonance. The London Millennium Bridge delivered the modern object lesson: it opened in
    June 2000, and synchronised lateral footfall — pedestrians unconsciously adjusting their
    gait to the deck's slight sway — set the deck oscillating sideways within hours, forcing a
    closure and a two-year retrofit of dampers. The bridge was never under strength limits; it
    was a dynamics problem, and the fix was to break the rhythm, not to add steel.
  </p>
  <p>
    The numbers involved are small enough to work by hand. Model a timber office floor as a mass
    of 2,000 kg riding on a stiffness of 3.2 MN per metre. Its natural frequency is
    f = (1 ÷ 2π) × √(k ÷ m) = (1 ÷ 2π) × √1,600 ≈ 6.4 Hz. Pedestrians walk at roughly 1.6 to
    2.4 steps per second, and footfall forces carry harmonics at two and three times that rate,
    so a 6.4 Hz floor sits above the second harmonic of a brisk walk, but not by a comfortable
    margin — which is why traditional guidance pushes floors under rhythmic occupancy (gyms,
    dance halls) above roughly 7.5 Hz: not so they are stronger, but so the crowd cannot find
    their rhythm. The same calculation, run in the lateral direction, is what the Millennium
    Bridge's designers under-weighted.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The three-equations toolkit has a hard limit: <em>statically indeterminate</em> structures
    — a continuous beam over several supports, a rigid frame — have more unknowns than
    equilibrium equations. Closing them requires the material's stiffness and compatibility of
    deformation: members must deflect together, and stiffer members attract more load. This is
    why indeterminate analysis (the force method and displacement method, and today the finite
    element method) sits at the gateway from second-year mechanics to real design, and why
    differential settlement, which redistributes load between redundant supports, matters far
    more than novices expect.
  </p>
  <p>
    Moment of inertia returns at full power in stability. A slender column under compression
    does not fail by crushing but by bowing sideways, at the Euler load P<sub>cr</sub> =
    π²EI ÷ L² — notice I appearing again, and notice the square: halving the effective length
    quarters the buckling capacity. Design codes wrap this in slenderness ratios and
    imperfection factors. On the dynamics side, the modern toolkit is the response spectrum
    (for earthquakes, the peak response a structure of each natural period can expect from a
    design ground motion), influence lines (a plot of how a stationary load at each position
    affects one response — swept along by a moving vehicle), and serviceability criteria that
    limit vibration even when strength is comfortable. Open problems are lively: human-induced
    vibration of long spans, loads that interact with the structure's own motion, and the
    shift from component code-checking to whole-building performance-based design under
    climate-driven wind and flood loads.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCEES FE Civil exam specifications; Hibbeler, <em>Engineering Mechanics: Statics &amp; Dynamics</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
